  "architecturalPlan": {
    "scaleRationale": "Explicit requirements are a contemporary tropical luxury villa in Saint Lucia, on a flat sea-view property, with sophisticated residential wings, distinctive roof character, generous glazing, a formal entrance, pool, deck, and shaded entertaining. The design therefore uses a substantial single-level 274 m² enclosed composition rather than adding unsupported upper levels: a 162 m² communal great-room pavilion is paired with two 56 m² private suite wings. Assumptions: the sea is toward local +Z, landward arrival is from -Z, and the climate benefits from deep shade and cross-ventilation opportunities. The budget/value is treated as a mandate for crafted proportions, layered outdoor rooms, and durable material contrast rather than as an area formula.",
    "arrivalAndCirculation": "A landward arrival reaches the limestone threshold plate and a protected rear entrance at the centre of the communal pavilion. A continuous open-air gallery along the rear façade links this entry to the two independently accessible suite-wing doors, separating private-room circulation from the sea-facing entertaining terrace. The principal living pavilion opens toward +Z to the sea terrace; the curved lounge pavilion is approached dry from that terrace and faces +Z. The residence is single-level, so no stair or lift circulation is required.",
    "volumeHierarchy": "The primary volume is main-living at [0, 0.1, 0], 18 m wide by 9 m deep, with a 3.6 m enclosure and a 1.4 m gable roof rise. Two quieter, low flat-roofed suite volumes sit at [-13, 0.1, -9] and [13, 0.1, -9], each 8 m by 7 m. They are held 1 m behind the primary pavilion, allowing a 26 m long arrival gallery at datum Y=0.2. The sea terrace occupies [0, 0.1, 7.5], 18 m by 6 m, extending directly from the main pavilion; the pool is beyond it at [4, 0.15, 11].",
    "relationshipsAndViews": "The assumed principal sea outlook is +Z. Five articulated glazed bays across the living pavilion face the sea terrace, with solid side and rear walls retaining privacy and furniture walls. The terrace begins at the living threshold and carries the open elliptical lounge pavilion on its western side, while a dry approach runs eastward to the rectangular pool's coordinated deck. The private suites face the arrival gallery rather than the pool, giving them a calm, protected orientation while remaining connected to the main residence.",
    "roofFacadeAndMaterials": "A restrained gable roof gives the communal pavilion its high, memorable silhouette; the two suite wings use lower flat roofs as deliberately recessive companions. The shaded entertainment pavilion provides the only curved roof expression, avoiding repetitive roof forms. Ivory enclosure walls, limestone terrace and pool finishes, bronze-framed generated glazing, and timber roof soffits create a warm coastal palette. The living façade is rhythmically divided into five deep glazed bays rather than a single undifferentiated glass wall; the entry is framed by limestone portal piers and a separate flat canopy.",
    "intendedSpaces": [
      "Sea-facing communal living, dining, and flexible kitchen zone within the main pavilion",
      "Two private single-room bedroom-suite wings, each with a glazed entry and protected solid enclosure",
      "Landward arrival gallery and formal covered threshold",
      "Large limestone sea terrace for outdoor dining and lounging",
      "Open curved shaded entertainment lounge pavilion",
      "Rectangular swimming pool with generated basin, coping, dry circuit, and connected approach"
    ],
